tice or in any of the federal courts  in any of the 50 states around the  country in those systems people are  released prior to trial without having  to pay any money unless the  that they're a danger to the community  or a risk of flight and so in the  federal system and in DC decisions about  whether a human being is kept in a cage  prior to trial are made based on  evidence and about whether they're a  danger rather than based on someone's  poverty but in all of the other states  whether you stay in jail after you're  arrested by the police  depends solely on whether you can afford  to pay bail and now explain how the bail  bond system works as you mentioned your  ability to pay that bail may vary  drastically and it often does so talk  about what service a bail bondsman  provides to someone who has been set a  bail realistically that they cannot  afford so in 46 states we have what's  called the commercial bail bond industry  there are four states that have outlawed  bail bondsman the United States and the  Philippines are the only two countries  in the world that still allow commercial  bail bondsman what this industry does is  it preys upon the people that are too  poor to afford to pay the bail that's  been set by the court they come in and  they say if you give us a percentage of  that amount we will tell the court that  we'll cover the rest  now they typically because they control  so many state legislators and local  government systems they've typically  passed rules that actually protect them  even from having to pay at all even if  you don't show up there Whiskey  virtually nothing what they then do is  they trap these people in these  incredibly onerous exploitative  contracts so sometimes they might say  you give us five percent of the money  bail and then you agree to pay us the  rest at the highest allowable interest  rate under law for years so you could be  like some of our clients you could be  arrested by the police and then you  could be held in jail on a bond you  can't afford and then you go to a bail  bondsman you make a payment and now you  owe them money for years and the very  next day the prosecutor looks at your  case and says there's no crime here or  there's not enough evidence and they  dismiss the case now you're stuck owing  money to the bail bondsman for a case  that was never prosecuted and this is  the way in which the bail bond industry  transfers billions of dollars every year  from the poorest people in our society  those people that are accused of crimes  and their families who can't afford  bale to some of the richest people in  the country the bail bond industry and  the insurance companies that underwrite  them one of the incredible circumstances  of this industry is that on the one hand  the bail bonds industry sometimes likes  to defend itself by saying listen if  you're sitting there in pretrial  detention it's going to be tougher for  you to build the strongest case possible  to defend yourself when your court date  ultimately comes at the same time though  Alec that same industry is lobbying all  the time  to put more alleged crimes under the  list qualifying for pretrial detention  unless you are able to pay bail they're  sort of creating a bigger and bigger  market for themselves aren't they they  are there are about 500,000 human beings  on any given night in this country who  are in a jail cell because they cannot  afford to pay bail that's the way the  bail industry likes it they want as many  people as possible as potential  customers so they're constantly lobbying  state and local governments for more or  another incredible thing that they do is  they lobby state and local governments  for other policies that subtly help them  so for example in the state of Louisiana  if I am arrested and brought to court  for a bail hearing the public defender  that represents me the prosecutor that's  arguing against me the sheriff that  brought me there and the judge that is  making a decision about my bail all take  by statute a percentage cut of the money  bail that's set in my case and that  statute was put into place by the bail  bond industry to give everyone in the  system an incentive to keep that bail  bond industries thriving and that's the  kind of perverse incentives that we're  seeing all over the country with the  bail bond industry now and it was really  okay go ahead yeah no no I was gonna say  what's really interesting about the  point you just made is the bail bond  industry likes to advertise itself as  helping to release people and yet in our  lawsuits after we've seen them filed we  see a lot of fear-mongering by the dáil  bond industry about about how well if  you if you get rid of the bail bond  industry you're gonna really be leasing  all these criminals which is their the  very advertising slogan that they've  been going by for years what's  fascinating about it and the reason why  a lot of police and prosecutors and  sheriffs are really opposed to the bail  industry is that if you believe that a  person is a danger to the community  and you think that person could harm  other people while they're awaiting  trial why in the world would you release  that person just because they have money  if someone is a dangerous predator I  would argue they're even more dangerous  if they have resources and money so the  bail bond industry is advocating for a  system that releases Robert Durst  but that cages Sandra bland and that is  absolutely absurd do you have data  nationally on what percentage of alleged  perpetrators are not even offered bail  in other words they're considered such a  risk to society that bail is not even an  option and then how many are offered  bail but ultimately never pay it either  directly or via bail bondsmen and how  many do pay the bail and get out do we  have some sort of sense of those numbers  no and in fact no one's keeping those  numbers precisely because of incentives  involved here and the bail bond industry  doesn't want there to be decisions made  about people's dangerousness they want  people to be offered high money bonds on  the chance they could get the money  together to pay it so when when courts  all around the country in most states  aren't ever making the determination  that someone is dangerous they're just  estimating if a judge thinks that a  person is dangerous in a state that uses  a lot of commercial money bail they'll  just estimate an amount of money that  they think the person can't afford so  they'll say you know a million dollars  on the hope that the person can't pay it  you know that very unconstitutional  obviously but that's what's happening in  courts all over the country and so no  one is keeping any kind of data right  now on the question that you asked which  is sort of what's happening to people  that are that are really dangerous and  are they being released on bail because  they're rich and things like that so we  don't really know all we know is that  the money bail system is keeping  hundreds of thousands of very low-risk  nonviolent and and otherwise and  offenders that we know through the data  aren't likely to commit new offenses why  bother pending trial in jail just  because they're poor I want to go back  to something you sort of alluded to  which is the sort of general lack of  risk that bail bondsman have for losing  money they claim right that they deserve  to earn this sort of commission  typically ten percent for helping people  get out of pretrial detention because  they are taking on risk right if the  alleged perpetrator  disappears after they've been bailed out  by the bail bondsmen bail bondsman is on  the hook for their entire bail the  reality though is that these bail  bondsman individually have insurance and  that if you look at the numbers it is  very rare that they actually are out  anything close to 100% of the bail for  any individual alleged perpetrator they  have a very solid insurance system in  place it's very true all of those  empirical claims of the bond industry  are complete fabrications the other  really interesting point is what they've  been ago she ated for it a lot of states  and a lot of jurisdictions is a grace  period so they don't have to even if the  person does skip out they don't have to  make that payment for two three four  years right and so what you see then is  they rely on the police it's very very  common in our society that the police  will then pick somebody up on a warrant  because we've got the US Marshals and  local police and sheriff's who are out  looking for people if they ever skip  court and when those people are then  brought back in by the police not by the  bail industry the bail industry doesn't  have to pay so they sort of freeride on  law enforcement to to assure themselves  of never having to put any risk up and  as you say even on the very very very  small number of cases where they are  exposed because they the person skipped  court they have insurance to protect  themselves from that and it's actually  those insurance companies not individual  bail bonds people but those insurance  companies that are providing a lot of  the lobbying and the money and the  financial support for a lot of these  these terrible policies and I think it's  yet another example it's yet another  example where there's sort of capitalism  for the profits while you socialize the  risk that's exactly right and and what's  really encouraging now is you're seeing  law enforcement leaders all over the  country the Department of Justice  federal prosecutors local sheriff's  national associations of police officers  who are speaking out against this  because the system makes absolutely no  sense it does not make sense to release  people who might be dangerous just  because they're rich it does not make  sense to hold people in a cage who might  not oh that's because they're poor we've  been speaking with Alec Carrick it's on  a civil rights lawyer thanks so much for  talking to us about this really  important issue  thanks for having me

